                "text": "**Analyzing Shape Extensions**\n\nI'm currently focused on how shapes, specifically pixels, extend and interact. For instance, I'm examining the scenario where a pixel with value '2' at (0,3) appears to extend downwards. I'm trying to determine the rules that govern these extensions, like where this extension ends. I am also considering how to represent these extended segments.\n\n\n**Investigating Extension Criteria**\n\nI'm now investigating what causes an entity to extend. Initial observations suggest that alignment with a larger block is crucial. For instance, the values '2' and '6' extended because their rows and columns intersected with the block, whereas '1' did not extend. The size of the connected component appears to play a role, as a block with a larger size is a good target for extension. Further testing is needed.\n\n\n**Defining Extension Rules**\n\nNow, I'm defining the extension rules based on observed alignment. The largest connected component, or the largest block, is central. A pixel or component extends if any of its pixels project a ray (up, down, left, right) towards this largest block, extending along the grid line until it touches. I'm noting the straight-line extension towards the target. In example 1, (0,3) for 2 shoots a ray down towards the block. Similarly, 6 at (3,7) shoots a ray left.\n\n\n",
